WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:01.600
Attention.

2
00:00:01.900 --> 00:00:06.003
This is the idea that unlocked everything after twenty seventeen.

3
00:00:06.003 --> 00:00:07.833
First, the problem it solved.

4
00:00:09.033 --> 00:00:12.267
Before twenty fourteen, sequence models had a bottleneck.

5
00:00:12.567 --> 00:00:14.633
The encoder read the entire input.

6
00:00:14.933 --> 00:00:17.711
And squeezed it into one fixed-size vector.

7
00:00:17.711 --> 00:00:22.233
Imagine summarizing a full mission log in five hundred twelve numbers.

8
00:00:22.533 --> 00:00:27.800
The decoder had to rebuild everything from that snapshot, and long inputs degraded badly.

9
00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:32.600
Attention, from Bahdanau and colleagues, removed the bottleneck.

10
00:00:32.900 --> 00:00:37.067
At every output step, the decoder looks back at all the input positions.

11
00:00:37.367 --> 00:00:41.700
And takes a weighted average, with weights the model learns to compute on the fly.

12
00:00:42.900 --> 00:00:44.700
The mechanism is three steps.

13
00:00:45.000 --> 00:00:48.376
First, score the query against every stored input.

14
00:00:48.376 --> 00:00:51.752
Luong and transformer attention use a dot product.

15
00:00:51.752 --> 00:00:55.600
Bahdanau's original used a small learned additive scorer.

16
00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:59.233
Then softmax turns those scores into weights that sum to one.

17
00:00:59.533 --> 00:01:02.480
And the weighted sum of the inputs is the context.

18
00:01:02.480 --> 00:01:07.667
It's a differentiable soft lookup, so the model learns where to look by backpropagation.

19
00:01:08.867 --> 00:01:11.533
Here is a tiny example with three stored states.

20
00:01:11.833 --> 00:01:16.202
The scores turn into weights: sixty four, ten, and twenty six percent.

21
00:01:16.202 --> 00:01:17.700
They sum to one hundred.

22
00:01:18.000 --> 00:01:22.233
Most of the weight lands on state zero, the one that best matches the query.

23
00:01:23.433 --> 00:01:25.033
Here's the same thing in code.

24
00:01:25.333 --> 00:01:27.833
Three stored encoder states, four numbers each.

25
00:01:28.133 --> 00:01:30.733
One query, what the decoder needs right now.

26
00:01:31.033 --> 00:01:36.501
We score with a matrix product, softmax the scores, and blend the states with those weights.

27
00:01:36.501 --> 00:01:37.333
No bottleneck.

28
00:01:38.533 --> 00:01:40.300
Here's why this matters for RAG.

29
00:01:40.600 --> 00:01:43.267
You stuff five reranked chunks into the prompt.

30
00:01:43.567 --> 00:01:51.000
When the model writes the anomaly began at a timestamp, it attends to the two chunks containing it, nearly ignoring the other three.

31
00:01:51.300 --> 00:01:53.933
It looks up the right one for every output token.

32
00:01:55.133 --> 00:01:55.912
Let's recap.

33
00:01:55.912 --> 00:01:58.767
Attention removes the one-vector bottleneck.

34
00:01:59.067 --> 00:02:00.967
Score, softmax, weighted sum.

35
00:02:01.267 --> 00:02:06.218
It's a differentiable soft lookup, and the weights are learned by backpropagation.

36
00:02:06.218 --> 00:02:07.667
Next up: self-attention.
